  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\,\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994895/assets/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{\u00a0}{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{\u00a0}\\text{and}\\text{\u00a0}b=2\\pi .$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
